2. Does Bethe Hessian apply to the constructed bipartite graphs?
Mathematical construction valid; community-count interpretation requires qualification
There are two different things here.

First, the construction itself is perfectly valid. Given a rectangular binary matrix \(X\), the matrix
\[
A=
\begin{pmatrix}
0 & X\\
X^\top & 0
\end{pmatrix}
\]

is a symmetric adjacency matrix of an undirected bipartite graph. Its degree matrix \(D\) is well-defined, and the resulting Bethe Hessian is a real symmetric matrix. There is no mathematical obstacle to calculating its inertia.

Second, the interpretation of its negative eigenvalues is more complicated.

The original Bethe Hessian community-detection argument was developed primarily for the ordinary stochastic block model. Saade et al. distinguish assortative and disassortative structures and use negative eigenvalues of \(H(r)\) and \(H(-r)\) to identify them. 

papers.neurips.cc




Your rectangular matrices represent two-mode networks. Their community structure is not necessarily the same as ordinary assortative community structure.

For example, consider author × article. A meaningful community could consist of a group of authors and a group of articles that are strongly associated with each other. Two authors in that community are not directly connected in the bipartite graph. Their relationship is expressed through shared article connections.

This is a different statistical structure. There is a dedicated literature on bipartite stochastic block models precisely because the community structure in bipartite networks differs from ordinary assortative communities. 

